window.EXAMDATA=window.EXAMDATA||{lessons:[],qs:[],terms:[]};
EXAMDATA.lessons.push(
 {"id":"FL-2.1.1","ch":2,"k":"K2","title":"How the chosen lifecycle affects testing","plain":"A software development lifecycle (SDLC) is the way a team organises its work. Sequential models (waterfall, V-model) do the phases one after another, so testing mostly happens late. Iterative and incremental models (Scrum, Kanban) deliver small working pieces again and again, so testing happens in every iteration. The model you pick shapes when and how you test.","coffee":"A sequential shop designs the whole menu, builds the whole counter, then tastes everything at opening. An iterative shop launches one drink at a time and tastes each new drink every week, re-checking the old ones too.","points":["Sequential (waterfall, V-model): activities follow each other; test execution mostly happens in later phases","Iterative/incremental (Scrum, Kanban): each iteration delivers a working increment, so testing is repeated every iteration","Regression testing grows in importance when the product changes every iteration","The chosen SDLC influences scope and timing of test activities (levels, types), detail of documentation, choice of techniques and approach, degree of automation, and tester roles"],"trap":"Do not assume the model removes test levels or testing; it changes the timing, detail and automation of testing, not whether you test."},
 {"id":"FL-2.1.2","ch":2,"k":"K1","title":"Good testing practices for every lifecycle","plain":"Some testing habits are good no matter which lifecycle you use. Every development activity has a matching test activity, so every piece of work gets quality-checked. Each test level has its own objectives, and test analysis and design for a level start while the matching development activity is happening. Testers join discussions about requirements and design, and review drafts as soon as they exist.","coffee":"Like a barista who checks the milk, the beans and the machine at each step of opening the shop, and who reads the new menu draft before it is printed rather than after.","points":["Every development activity has a corresponding test activity","Each test level has specific objectives suited to that level","Test analysis and design for a level begin during the matching development activity","Testers take part in refining requirements and design","Testers review work product drafts as soon as they are available"],"trap":"Waiting for a finished, approved document before starting test design is the opposite of the good practice, which is to start early."},
 {"id":"FL-2.1.3","ch":2,"k":"K1","title":"Test-first approaches: TDD, ATDD, BDD","plain":"Test-first approaches write the tests before the code that they check. Test-driven development (TDD) has developers write component tests first, then code to pass them, then refactor. Acceptance test-driven development (ATDD) derives tests from acceptance criteria while the system is being designed, before the code. Behaviour-driven development (BDD) describes desired behaviour in natural language, often as Given/When/Then, which is then turned into executable tests.","coffee":"Before opening a new drink, you write the taste checklist first (TDD/ATDD style), or describe it in plain words like Given a flat white order, When it is served, Then it is hot and has a smooth foam (BDD style).","points":["TDD: write tests first, then code that satisfies them, then refactor","ATDD: tests come from acceptance criteria, during system design, before implementation","BDD: behaviour written in natural language, often Given/When/Then, then automated","In all three the tests drive the development"],"trap":"Do not mix them up: TDD is developer-level tests, ATDD starts from acceptance criteria, BDD is natural-language behaviour."},
 {"id":"FL-2.1.4","ch":2,"k":"K2","title":"DevOps and testing","plain":"DevOps joins development and operations so software can be built, tested and released continuously. This gives fast feedback on quality, supports shift-left because continuous integration encourages quality code with component tests, and builds stable test environments automatically. It also makes non-functional characteristics such as performance and reliability more visible. The pipeline and its tools must be set up and maintained, and manual testing from the user's viewpoint is still needed.","coffee":"Like a shop where the order screen, kitchen and delivery riders work as one flow with instant feedback, instead of three teams passing notes.","points":["Benefits: fast feedback on code quality, CI promotes shift-left, CI/CD helps create stable test environments","Greater visibility of non-functional characteristics (e.g. performance efficiency, reliability)","Pipeline automation reduces repetitive manual testing and minimises regression risk","Risks: the delivery pipeline and CI/CD tools must be defined, set up and maintained","Test automation needs extra resources and can be hard to establish","Manual testing, especially from a user's point of view, is still needed"],"trap":"DevOps does not eliminate manual testing."},
 {"id":"FL-2.1.5","ch":2,"k":"K2","title":"The shift-left approach","plain":"Shift-left means doing testing activities earlier in the lifecycle, instead of waiting until the end. It does not mean testing later is dropped. Examples are reviewing specifications from a tester's view, writing tests before the code, using CI/CD, running static analysis before dynamic testing, and starting non-functional testing at component level. It may need extra training and effort up front, but is expected to save effort and cost later.","coffee":"Tasting the milk before steaming it, instead of waiting for a customer to complain about the finished cappuccino.","points":["Test activities start earlier in the lifecycle","It does not mean later testing is neglected","Examples: reviewing specs from a test view, tests before code, CI/CD, static analysis before dynamic testing, non-functional tests starting at component level","May cost extra training, effort and money early on, expected to save later"],"trap":"Shift-left means earlier, not instead of later testing."},
 {"id":"FL-2.1.6","ch":2,"k":"K2","title":"Retrospectives and process improvement","plain":"A retrospective is a meeting where the team looks back and decides how to improve. It is held at the end of a project, iteration or release, or whenever needed. Typical topics are what went well and should be kept, what did not go well and could improve, and how to put improvements into practice. Outcomes are recorded and are typically part of the test completion report.","coffee":"The Monday huddle where the baristas say: the new grinder routine worked, the queue at 8am didn't, and here is what we will try this week.","points":["Held at the end of a project, iteration or release, or when needed","Typical topics: what was successful, what was not, how to improve and keep successes","Results are recorded and are typically part of the test completion report","Benefits: better test effectiveness and efficiency, better testware, team bonding and learning, better test basis quality, better cooperation between development and testing"],"trap":"A retrospective is about improving the process, not blaming individuals or approving a release."},
 {"id":"FL-2.2.1","ch":2,"k":"K2","title":"Test levels","plain":"Test levels are groups of test activities organised together. Component testing checks individual components in isolation, often with stubs and drivers. Component integration testing checks how components interact. System testing checks the whole system, including end-to-end tasks and non-functional behaviour. System integration testing checks interfaces to other systems and external services. Acceptance testing checks readiness for deployment.","coffee":"Component = testing the grinder alone. Component integration = grinder plus machine working together. System = the whole shop serving an order from till to cup. System integration = the shop's till talking to the card payment provider. Acceptance = the owner or customers confirming it's what they wanted.","points":["Component testing: components in isolation, often by developers, often with stubs/drivers/mocks","Component integration testing: interfaces and interactions between components","System testing: overall behaviour of the whole system, incl. end-to-end tasks and non-functional behaviour","System integration testing: interfaces between the system and other systems or external services","Acceptance forms: user acceptance (UAT), operational acceptance (OAT, e.g. backup/restore, disaster recovery, user management), contractual and regulatory acceptance, alpha and beta testing","Alpha: at the developing organisation's site; beta: at the customers' or users' own locations"],"trap":"Component integration (inside the system) is not system integration (between the system and other systems)."},
 {"id":"FL-2.2.2","ch":2,"k":"K2","title":"Test types","plain":"A test type is a group of test activities aimed at specific quality characteristics. Functional testing checks what the system should do. Non-functional testing checks how well it does it, for example performance, usability, security, reliability. Black-box testing derives tests from specifications and behaviour, without looking at internals. White-box testing derives tests from the internal structure, such as code or architecture. Types can be applied at all test levels.","coffee":"Functional: does the till add up the total right? Non-functional: does it do so in under 2 seconds at lunch rush? Black-box: tasting the coffee. White-box: inspecting the machine's plumbing.","points":["Functional testing: what the system should do","Non-functional testing: how well the system behaves (e.g. performance efficiency, usability, security, reliability)","Black-box: based on specifications, not on internal structure","White-box: based on internal structure (code, architecture, data flows)","Test types can be performed at all test levels"],"trap":"Non-functional testing is not only done at the end; it can start as early as component level."},
 {"id":"FL-2.2.3","ch":2,"k":"K2","title":"Confirmation testing vs regression testing","plain":"After a defect is fixed, confirmation testing checks that the fix worked, usually by re-running the test that failed. Regression testing checks that a change (a fix or anything else) did not break something else, in the same component, other components, or even connected systems. Regression suites are run again and again and change slowly, so they are good candidates for automation. Impact analysis helps decide how much regression testing is needed.","coffee":"You fix the milk steamer. Confirmation: make the cappuccino that failed again. Regression: make a flat white, a latte and a mocha to be sure the repair didn't spoil them.","points":["Confirmation testing: checks that a fix resolved the original defect, typically re-running failed tests","Regression testing: checks for unintended side effects of a change","Regression can affect the same component, other components and other systems","Regression suites are run often and are good automation candidates","Both can be done at all test levels"],"trap":"Re-running the failed test is confirmation; running other, previously passing tests is regression."},
 {"id":"FL-2.3.1","ch":2,"k":"K2","title":"Maintenance testing and its triggers","plain":"Maintenance testing is testing done after software is already in use and changes. Its triggers are modification (enhancements, fixes, patches, upgrades), migration (moving to another platform or converting data) and retirement (end of life, often with data archiving). Impact analysis evaluates a change to find intended consequences, expected side effects and affected areas, so you know what to retest. Its scope depends on the risk of the change, the size of the system and the size of the change.","coffee":"A shop already trading changes its POS software (modification), moves to a new till brand (migration), or closes a branch and archives its sales records (retirement).","points":["Triggers: modification, migration, retirement","Modification: planned enhancements, corrective/emergency changes, environment or COTS upgrades, patches","Migration: new platform, includes testing the new environment and data conversion","Retirement: may include testing data archiving and restore/retrieval","Impact analysis finds intended consequences, side effects and affected areas","Scope depends on risk of the change, size of existing system, size of the change"],"trap":"Impact analysis is not itself a trigger; it is the tool used to decide what to test after a trigger."},
 {"id":"FL-3.1.1","ch":3,"k":"K1","title":"Work products static testing can examine","plain":"Static testing looks at work products without running the software. Almost any work product can be reviewed if the reviewers can read and understand it. Static analysis tools work on products with a formal structure, usually code or models. Dynamic things, like response time of a running system, cannot be examined statically.","coffee":"Proofreading the menu, the recipe cards and the supplier contract is static. Serving a customer to see how long the order takes is dynamic.","points":["Specifications: business, functional and security requirements","Epics, user stories and acceptance criteria","Architecture and design specifications, source code","Testware: test plans, test cases, test procedures, automated test scripts","User guides, web pages, contracts, project plans, schedules, budgets","Configuration and infrastructure set-up, models such as activity diagrams","Reviews: any work product reviewers can read; static analysis: work products with a formal structure and a suitable tool"],"trap":"Static testing is not limited to code; requirements, test cases and plans can be reviewed too."},
 {"id":"FL-3.1.2","ch":3,"k":"K2","title":"The value of static testing","plain":"Static testing finds defects early, before dynamic testing is possible and when they are cheap to fix. It can find things dynamic testing may miss, such as unreachable code. Reviewing requirements uncovers inconsistencies, ambiguities, contradictions, omissions, inaccuracies and redundancies. This improves quality, lowers cost and time of development and testing, and helps team communication.","coffee":"Catching a wrong price on the draft menu is far cheaper than reprinting 500 menus after customers complain.","points":["Finds defects in work products before dynamic testing","Finds defects dynamic testing may not, e.g. unreachable code","Prevents defects in design or coding by finding problems in requirements","Can improve productivity, and reduce development and testing cost and time","Can reduce total cost of quality over the software's lifetime","Improves communication between team members taking part in reviews"],"trap":"Static testing complements dynamic testing; it does not replace it."},
 {"id":"FL-3.1.3","ch":3,"k":"K2","title":"Static versus dynamic testing","plain":"Static testing finds defects directly in work products, without running anything. Dynamic testing runs the software and notices failures, from which defects are traced. The two find different kinds of defects. Static typically finds requirement problems, design defects, coding defects such as unused variables or unreachable code, standards deviations, wrong interface specifications and some security vulnerabilities. Dynamic is better for externally visible behaviour and things like performance.","coffee":"Reading the recipe finds a missing ingredient (static). Tasting the finished drink finds that it comes out too bitter (dynamic).","points":["Static: defects found directly in work products, no execution","Dynamic: software is executed, failures reveal defects","Static finds e.g. ambiguous requirements, unreachable code, unused variables, naming-standard breaches, interface mismatches","Static can find defects in non-executable work products","Static improves internal quality and consistency; dynamic focuses on externally visible behaviour","Dynamic is typically stronger for performance-type problems"],"trap":"Static testing finds defects directly; dynamic testing finds failures first."},
 {"id":"FL-3.2.1","ch":3,"k":"K1","title":"Early and frequent stakeholder feedback","plain":"Getting feedback from stakeholders early and often lets quality problems be raised early. Without it, the product may not match what stakeholders wanted, causing rework, missed deadlines, blame and even project failure. Frequent feedback prevents misunderstandings about requirements and lets changes be understood and built earlier. It helps the team focus on the features with most value and the biggest effect on risks.","coffee":"Letting regulars taste the new blend while you are still developing it, rather than launching and learning nobody likes it.","points":["Allows early communication of potential quality problems","Prevents misunderstandings of requirements","Changes to requirements are understood and implemented earlier","Team better understands what to build and focuses on the most valuable features","Lack of feedback can cause costly rework, missed deadlines, blame games or project failure"],"trap":"Feedback does not guarantee a defect-free product; it reduces misunderstanding and rework."},
 {"id":"FL-3.2.2","ch":3,"k":"K2","title":"The review process activities","plain":"A review has five activities. Planning defines scope, purpose and exit criteria. Review initiation gets everyone and everything ready. In individual review each reviewer inspects the product alone and logs anomalies, recommendations and questions. Communication and analysis discusses the anomalies, decides status, ownership and actions. Fixing and reporting creates defect reports, tracks fixes and reports results.","coffee":"Planning: decide to check the new menu. Initiation: hand out copies. Individual review: each barista marks mistakes. Communication: team meets to decide which are real. Fixing: the printer corrects it and you note the result.","points":["1 Planning: scope, purpose, work product, quality characteristics, exit criteria, effort and timeframe","2 Review initiation: participants have the work product, understand roles, have what they need","3 Individual review: each reviewer logs anomalies, recommendations, questions","4 Communication and analysis: anomalies are discussed; decide status, ownership and actions (often in a meeting)","5 Fixing and reporting: defect reports for defects, follow up fixes, report review results, accept work product once exit criteria met","An anomaly is not necessarily a defect"],"trap":"Reviewers log anomalies alone during individual review; the discussion belongs to communication and analysis."},
 {"id":"FL-3.2.3","ch":3,"k":"K1","title":"Roles and responsibilities in reviews","plain":"The manager decides what is reviewed and provides staff and time. The author creates and fixes the work product. The moderator (facilitator) keeps review meetings effective, handling mediation, timing and a safe atmosphere. The scribe (recorder) collates anomalies and records decisions. Reviewers do the reviewing. The review leader takes overall responsibility, such as choosing the participants and organising when and where. In some review types one person may play several roles.","coffee":"Owner decides the menu is reviewed (manager), the menu writer (author), the shift lead who keeps the huddle on time (moderator), the person with the notepad (scribe), the baristas reading it (reviewers).","points":["Manager: decides what is reviewed, provides resources","Author: creates and fixes the work product","Moderator/facilitator: runs effective meetings, mediation, time, safe environment","Scribe/recorder: collates anomalies, records decisions","Reviewers: perform the review; project staff, subject matter experts or other stakeholders","Review leader: overall responsibility, decides who is involved, when and where"],"trap":"The scribe records; the moderator facilitates; the review leader organises. Do not swap them."},
 {"id":"FL-3.2.4","ch":3,"k":"K2","title":"Review types","plain":"There are four main review types, from least to most formal. An informal review has no formal documented process and may not need a meeting. A walkthrough is led by the author and can educate reviewers or build consensus; an individual review beforehand is optional. A technical review is done by technically qualified reviewers and led by a moderator, aiming at consensus and decisions on a technical problem. An inspection is the most formal: it follows the complete review process, aims to find the maximum number of anomalies, and collects metrics to improve the process.","coffee":"Informal: ask a colleague to glance at your menu. Walkthrough: you talk the team through it. Technical review: the senior barista and equipment expert examine it. Inspection: a formal check with rules and records.","points":["Informal review: follows no defined process, needs no formal documented output, main goal is to detect anomalies","Walkthrough: led by the author; objectives include educating, consensus, generating ideas, detecting anomalies; prior individual review is optional","Technical review: technically qualified reviewers, led by a moderator; main aim is consensus and decisions on a technical problem, also finding anomalies","Inspection: most formal; follows the complete generic review process; main aim is finding the maximum number of anomalies; metrics are collected and used to improve the SDLC and the inspection process; the author cannot be review leader or scribe","Review types can be mixed; choose based on objectives and context"],"trap":"The author leads a walkthrough, but in an inspection the author cannot act as review leader or scribe."},
 {"id":"FL-3.2.5","ch":3,"k":"K1","title":"Success factors for reviews","plain":"Reviews work better when objectives are clear and exit criteria are measurable. Choose a review type that suits the work product and people, and review in small chunks so concentration stays high. Give participants enough preparation time, give feedback to stakeholders and authors, and train everyone. Management support and a review-friendly culture matter, and meetings must be facilitated well.","coffee":"Checking one section of the menu at a time, with enough time to read it first, beats a 3-hour marathon on the whole manual.","points":["Clear objectives and measurable exit criteria (evaluating participants is never an objective)","Appropriate review type for objectives, work product, participants and context","Reviews in small chunks to keep concentration","Feedback to stakeholders and authors so they can improve","Adequate preparation time, management support, training for participants","Making reviews part of the organisation's culture; well-facilitated meetings"],"trap":"Bigger sessions do not help: small chunks keep reviewers focused."}
);
EXAMDATA.qs.push(
 {id:'c2-001',ch:2,lo:'FL-2.1.2',k:'K1',q:'Which statement describes a good testing practice that applies in every software development lifecycle?',o:['All test levels share one common set of objectives','Test design for a level starts only once its code is finished','Every development activity has a corresponding test activity','Only the test team may review drafts of requirements'],a:2,why:'Correct: a matching test activity for every development activity is a lifecycle-independent good practice. Wrong: one shared set of objectives ignores that each level has its own; starting test design after the code is finished is too late, since it should begin during the matching development activity; limiting draft reviews to testers is wrong because testers join in reviewing early drafts rather than owning them alone.'},
 {id:'c2-002',ch:2,lo:'FL-2.1.2',k:'K1',q:'According to good testing practices, when should test analysis and design for a test level begin?',o:['After the code freeze for the release is declared','Once test execution at the previous level has completely ended','While the matching development activity is under way','After the software has been released to production'],a:2,why:'Correct: test analysis and design start while the corresponding development activity is in progress. Wrong: after code freeze, after the previous level has finished executing, and after release all describe starting far too late.'},
 {id:'c2-003',ch:2,lo:'FL-2.1.2',k:'K1',q:'Which statement describes how testers should be involved in requirements and design work?',o:['Testers wait for formal approval of the documents before reading them','Testers get involved only when the first build reaches the test environment','Testers take part in code reviews only, not in requirements discussions','Testers join requirements and design discussions and review early drafts'],a:3,why:'Correct: testers take part in refining requirements and design and review work product drafts as soon as they are available. Wrong: waiting for approval and waiting for the first build are both too late; limiting testers to code reviews wrongly excludes requirements and design discussions.'},
 {id:'c2-004',ch:2,lo:'FL-2.1.2',k:'K1',q:'Which statement about test levels is a good practice in any lifecycle?',o:['Objectives are decided only by the end users at acceptance time','Each test level has specific objectives suited to that level','Each level repeats exactly the objectives of the level below it','The lifecycle model sets the objectives, so level-specific ones are unnecessary'],a:1,why:'Correct: each test level should have specific objectives appropriate to that level. Wrong: leaving objectives to end users at acceptance ignores the other levels; repeating the lower level objectives wastes effort; the lifecycle model does not replace level-specific objectives.'},
 {id:'c2-005',ch:2,lo:'FL-2.1.2',k:'K1',q:'Which of the following is NOT a good testing practice that applies to all software development lifecycles?',o:['Postponing all test design until a build is stable in production','Reviewing work product drafts as soon as they are available','Matching each development activity with a test activity','Starting test design for a level while its development activity runs'],a:0,why:'Correct (the NOT answer): postponing all test design until production is far too late and contradicts early involvement. Wrong: early reviews of drafts, a test activity for every development activity, and starting test design during the matching development activity are all genuine good practices.'},
 {id:'c2-006',ch:2,lo:'FL-2.1.3',k:'K1',q:'Which approach has developers write automated component tests first, then write code that passes them, then refactor?',o:['Behaviour-driven development','Test-driven development','Acceptance test-driven development','Exploratory testing'],a:1,why:'Correct: that is the test-driven development cycle. Wrong: behaviour-driven development describes behaviour in natural language such as Given/When/Then; acceptance test-driven development derives tests from acceptance criteria; exploratory testing is not a test-first approach.'},
 {id:'c2-007',ch:2,lo:'FL-2.1.3',k:'K1',q:'In which test-first approach are tests derived from acceptance criteria during system design, before the code is written?',o:['Regression testing','Alpha testing','Acceptance test-driven development','Test-driven development'],a:2,why:'Correct: ATDD derives tests from acceptance criteria as part of design, before implementation. Wrong: regression testing re-checks after changes; alpha testing is an acceptance form done once software exists; TDD centres on developer-written component tests rather than acceptance criteria.'},
 {id:'c2-008',ch:2,lo:'FL-2.1.3',k:'K1',q:'Which test-first approach expresses the expected behaviour in natural language, commonly in a Given/When/Then format?',o:['Test-driven development','Acceptance test-driven development','Mutation testing','Behaviour-driven development'],a:3,why:'Correct: BDD describes desired behaviour in natural language and then turns it into executable tests. Wrong: TDD writes component tests first; ATDD starts from acceptance criteria; mutation testing is not a test-first approach.'},
 {id:'c2-009',ch:2,lo:'FL-2.1.3',k:'K1',q:'Which of the following is an example of a test-first approach to development?',o:['Test-driven development','Beta testing','Impact analysis','Big-bang integration'],a:0,why:'Correct: TDD writes tests before code. Wrong: beta testing is an acceptance form done with the existing software; impact analysis is used in maintenance testing; big-bang integration is an integration strategy; none of them is test-first.'},
 {id:'c2-010',ch:2,lo:'FL-2.1.3',k:'K1',q:'In test-driven development, which order of work is typical?',o:['Write the code first, then tests that simply describe what it does','Write tests first, write code to satisfy them, then refactor','Two separate teams write code and tests, then compare the results','Write acceptance tests only after the release'],a:1,why:'Correct: TDD writes tests first, then code to pass them, then refactors. Wrong: writing tests after the code reverses the order; two teams comparing results is not TDD; tests written after release are not test-first.'},
 {id:'c2-011',ch:2,lo:'FL-2.1.3',k:'K1',q:'Which list contains only test-first approaches to development?',o:['BDD, SIT, TDD','TDD, CI, CD','TDD, ATDD, BDD','ATDD, UAT, OAT'],a:2,why:'Correct: TDD, ATDD and BDD are the three test-first approaches. Wrong: SIT is system integration testing, a test level; CI and CD are integration and delivery practices; UAT and OAT are forms of acceptance testing.'},
 {id:'c2-012',ch:2,lo:'FL-2.1.1',k:'K2',q:'A team moves from a sequential model to Scrum with short iterations. Which impact on testing is most likely?',o:['Detailed formal test documentation is produced before each iteration starts','All testing moves to the final iteration just before the release date','Test levels are dropped, since each increment is tested once at the very end','Regression testing matters more, as each iteration changes the product'],a:3,why:'Correct: with frequent changes to an evolving product, regression testing gains importance. Wrong: iterative models typically use lighter documentation; testing happens in every iteration rather than only in the last; test levels are not dropped.'},
 {id:'c2-013',ch:2,lo:'FL-2.1.1',k:'K2',q:'Which statement best explains how the chosen lifecycle model affects testing?',o:['It shapes when and how testing is done, including documentation, techniques, automation and roles','It only determines which test tool is purchased, with no effect on documentation or techniques','It removes the need for non-functional testing because the model already covers quality','It decides which defects exist in the product, so the team cannot influence them'],a:0,why:'Correct: the model influences timing and scope of test activities, documentation detail, techniques, automation and tester roles. Wrong: it affects far more than tool purchasing; non-functional testing is still needed; defects come from human errors, not from the lifecycle model.'},
 {id:'c2-014',ch:2,lo:'FL-2.1.4',k:'K2',q:'Which statement describes a benefit of DevOps for testing?',o:['Manual testing from the user viewpoint is no longer needed','Fast quality feedback, and CI that promotes shift-left','Non-functional characteristics can be ignored because the pipeline checks them','A delivery pipeline appears with no set-up effort'],a:1,why:'Correct: DevOps gives fast quality feedback and continuous integration promotes shift-left. Wrong: manual testing from the user viewpoint is still needed; DevOps increases the visibility of non-functional characteristics rather than letting them be ignored; the pipeline must be defined and established.'},
 {id:'c2-015',ch:2,lo:'FL-2.1.4',k:'K2',q:'Which is a challenge a team may face when adopting DevOps for testing?',o:['Stable test environments become impossible to achieve when tests are automated','Regression testing can only be done manually in a pipeline-based flow','CI/CD tools must be introduced and maintained, and automation needs effort','Testers may no longer give feedback on quality once a pipeline is introduced'],a:2,why:'Correct: the pipeline, tools and automation must be established and maintained, which takes resources. Wrong: automated processes typically help create stable environments; DevOps reduces repetitive manual regression testing; testers still give quality feedback.'},
 {id:'c2-016',ch:2,lo:'FL-2.1.5',k:'K2',q:'Which is an example of the shift-left approach?',o:['Skipping reviews so that coding can begin sooner','Delaying static analysis until system testing is complete','Running performance tests only after release to customers','Writing tests from a user story before any code is written'],a:3,why:'Correct: designing tests before coding moves testing earlier. Wrong: skipping reviews removes early testing; delaying static analysis and running performance tests only after release both push testing later, the opposite of shift-left.'},
 {id:'c2-017',ch:2,lo:'FL-2.1.5',k:'K2',q:'Which statement best explains shift-left?',o:['Testing happens earlier; extra effort up front is expected to save effort later','Later testing is dropped as soon as early testing is in place','Only developers do the testing, so testers move on to other work','Dynamic testing is unnecessary because earlier checks are enough'],a:0,why:'Correct: shift-left starts testing earlier, accepting some early effort to save later. Wrong: later testing is not neglected; shift-left does not remove testers; dynamic testing remains needed.'},
 {id:'c2-018',ch:2,lo:'FL-2.1.6',k:'K2',q:'Which topic is typical for a retrospective?',o:['Who caused each defect, so the team can decide who to drop','What went well and should be kept, and what could be improved','Giving the formal sign-off for the production release this week','Re-running all the failed test cases'],a:1,why:'Correct: retrospectives look at successes, problems and how to improve. Wrong: the goal is process improvement, not blame; release approval is not its purpose; re-running tests is test execution.'},
 {id:'c2-019',ch:2,lo:'FL-2.1.6',k:'K2',q:'During an iteration the test environment kept crashing. How can a retrospective help?',o:['The crashes are logged as defects in the test basis','The next iteration is cancelled until the environment is fixed','The team discusses it and agrees improvements to follow up','The meeting itself repairs the environment without any follow-up'],a:2,why:'Correct: a retrospective lets the team discuss what went wrong and agree and follow up improvements. Wrong: environment crashes are not defects in the test basis; cancelling an iteration is not its purpose; a meeting does not repair systems by itself.'},
 {id:'c2-020',ch:2,lo:'FL-2.2.1',k:'K2',q:'Before go-live, administrators check backup and restore and disaster recovery of a new system. Which test level or form is this?',o:['Component integration testing','Beta testing','Contractual acceptance testing','Operational acceptance testing'],a:3,why:'Correct: backup/restore and disaster recovery checks are typical operational acceptance testing. Wrong: component integration checks interactions between components; beta testing is done by users at their own sites; contractual acceptance checks against contract criteria.'},
 {id:'c2-021',ch:2,lo:'FL-2.2.1',k:'K2',q:'A shop tests the link between its ordering system and an external card payment provider. Which test level is this?',o:['System integration testing','Component integration testing','Alpha testing','Component testing'],a:0,why:'Correct: interfaces between the system and external services belong to system integration testing. Wrong: component integration concerns interactions between components inside the system; alpha testing is a form of acceptance testing; component testing checks components in isolation.'},
 {id:'c2-022',ch:2,lo:'FL-2.2.1',k:'K2',q:'Which statement correctly distinguishes alpha testing from beta testing?',o:['Alpha is run at customers\' own premises; beta is run at the developer\'s site','Alpha is at the developer\'s site; beta is at customers\' or users\' locations','Alpha covers only performance checks, while beta covers only security checks','Alpha is a component test level, while beta is a system test level'],a:1,why:'Correct: alpha takes place at the developing organisation, beta at the customers\' or users\' own locations. Wrong: placing alpha at customer premises reverses them; splitting them by test type is invented; both are forms of acceptance testing, not component or system levels.'},
 {id:'c2-023',ch:2,lo:'FL-2.2.1',k:'K2',q:'Which statement correctly contrasts component testing with system testing?',o:['Component testing covers end-to-end tasks, while system testing covers isolated components','Component testing is done by end users, while system testing is done by developers','Component testing isolates parts, often with stubs or drivers; system testing covers the whole system','They are the same level, and the names depend on which team does the work'],a:2,why:'Correct: component testing isolates components, often using stubs or drivers, while system testing considers the behaviour of the whole system. Wrong: saying component testing covers end-to-end tasks reverses them; roles are not assigned that way; they are distinct test levels.'},
 {id:'c2-024',ch:2,lo:'FL-2.2.2',k:'K2',q:'Which of the following is an example of non-functional testing?',o:['Checking that a refund creates a credit note','Checking that a discount code reduces the order total','Checking that the till shows the correct total for a basket','Measuring how long search results take with 500 users online'],a:3,why:'Correct: response time under load tests how well the system performs. Wrong: credit notes, discount codes and basket totals all check what the system should do, so they are functional.'},
 {id:'c2-025',ch:2,lo:'FL-2.2.2',k:'K2',q:'Which statement correctly contrasts black-box and white-box testing?',o:['Black-box uses specified behaviour; white-box uses internal structure such as code','Black-box testing is only non-functional, and white-box testing is only functional','Both are applicable only at component test level, never at other levels','Black-box tests come from internal code; white-box tests come from the specification'],a:0,why:'Correct: black-box is based on behaviour and specifications, white-box on internal structure. Wrong: black-box can cover functional or non-functional aspects, and white-box is not limited to functional; both can be used at all levels; claiming black-box tests come from code reverses the two.'},
 {id:'c2-026',ch:2,lo:'FL-2.2.2',k:'K2',q:'A tester measures what percentage of the decision outcomes in the code were exercised by the tests. Which test type is this?',o:['Usability testing','White-box testing','Black-box testing','Confirmation testing'],a:1,why:'Correct: measuring coverage of code structure is white-box testing. Wrong: black-box ignores internal structure; usability is a non-functional characteristic unrelated to code coverage; confirmation testing checks that a defect fix worked.'},
 {id:'c2-027',ch:2,lo:'FL-2.2.3',k:'K2',q:'A defect in the discount calculation was fixed. The tester re-runs the test that originally failed. What is this?',o:['Impact analysis','Regression testing','Confirmation testing','Exploratory testing of the whole product'],a:2,why:'Correct: re-running a failed test to check the fix is confirmation testing. Wrong: impact analysis evaluates a change rather than executing tests; regression testing checks for side effects elsewhere; exploratory testing is not a fix check.'},
 {id:'c2-028',ch:2,lo:'FL-2.2.3',k:'K2',q:'After a fix, a tester runs previously passing tests in other modules to check nothing else broke. What is this?',o:['Component testing','Confirmation testing','Alpha testing','Regression testing'],a:3,why:'Correct: checking for unintended side effects of a change is regression testing. Wrong: component testing is a level, not a purpose; confirmation re-runs the failed test; alpha is an acceptance form.'},
 {id:'c2-029',ch:2,lo:'FL-2.2.3',k:'K2',q:'Why are regression test suites good candidates for test automation?',o:['They are run many times and change slowly','They never need any maintenance','They contain only new tests for new features','They can only be run by a tool'],a:0,why:'Correct: repeated runs of a stable suite make automation worthwhile. Wrong: suites still need maintenance; they mostly contain existing tests, not new-feature tests; they can also be run manually.'},
 {id:'c2-030',ch:2,lo:'FL-2.3.1',k:'K2',q:'An application is being decommissioned, but its data must be kept for ten years. Which trigger and test focus apply?',o:['Migration, testing the new platform and converting the data','Retirement, testing data archiving and the ability to restore data','Retirement, testing only response time on the old system','Modification, testing only the newest features before shutdown'],a:1,why:'Correct: retirement may include testing archiving and restore or retrieval of retained data. Wrong: nothing is being moved to a new platform, so it is not migration; response time is not the retirement concern; nothing is being modified.'},
 {id:'c2-031',ch:2,lo:'FL-2.3.1',k:'K2',q:'A company moves its working application from its own servers to a cloud platform with no feature changes. Which maintenance trigger is this?',o:['Modification, because the software itself is being changed','Retirement, because the old servers are being switched off','Migration, because the software moves to another platform','Impact analysis, because the change is being evaluated'],a:2,why:'Correct: moving to another platform is migration. Wrong: modification means changing the software, which is not happening here; retirement means the software reaches end of life; impact analysis is an activity, not a trigger.'},
 {id:'c2-032',ch:2,lo:'FL-2.3.1',k:'K2',q:'What is the purpose of impact analysis in maintenance testing?',o:['To decide which test automation tool the team should buy next year','To replace the need for regression testing after a change','To confirm that all earlier defects were fixed and closed','To find a change\'s consequences, side effects and affected areas'],a:3,why:'Correct: impact analysis evaluates a change to identify its consequences and the areas needing testing. Wrong: tool purchase is unrelated; it helps decide the extent of regression testing rather than replacing it; confirming earlier fixes is confirmation testing.'},
 {id:'c3-001',ch:3,lo:'FL-3.1.1',k:'K1',q:'Which of the following can be examined by static testing?',o:['The wording of a requirements specification','The frame rate while a game is running','How a login screen reacts to a wrong password','Memory usage while 200 users are logged in'],a:0,why:'Correct: a requirements specification is a work product that can be reviewed without executing anything. Wrong: frame rate, login behaviour and memory usage under load all need the software to run, which is dynamic testing.'},
 {id:'c3-002',ch:3,lo:'FL-3.1.1',k:'K1',q:'Static analysis tools can typically be applied to which kind of work product?',o:['Only informal documents, for example free-text meeting minutes','Work products with a formal structure, e.g. code or models','Only paper documents scanned from handwritten notes','Only an executable that is already running in production'],a:1,why:'Correct: static analysis works efficiently on work products with a formal structure, usually code or models. Wrong: free-text minutes and scanned notes lack formal structure; a running executable would be dynamic testing.'},
 {id:'c3-003',ch:3,lo:'FL-3.1.1',k:'K1',q:'Which set contains only work products that can be examined by static testing?',o:['CPU utilization, user guide, contract','Test procedures, response times, source code','User guide, test procedures, contract','Contract, memory leaks at runtime, user guide'],a:2,why:'Correct: a user guide, test procedures and a contract can all be reviewed without running software. Wrong: the other sets each include a runtime measurement (CPU use, response times, memory leaks) that needs execution.'},
 {id:'c3-004',ch:3,lo:'FL-3.2.1',k:'K1',q:'Which is a benefit of early and frequent stakeholder feedback?',o:['Reviewers can skip individual review because stakeholders already commented','Testing becomes unnecessary because stakeholders approved the requirements','It guarantees that no defects remain in the delivered product','Misunderstandings of requirements are prevented and changes land earlier'],a:3,why:'Correct: frequent feedback prevents misunderstandings and brings changes in earlier. Wrong: feedback does not replace individual review, does not remove the need for testing, and cannot guarantee zero defects.'},
 {id:'c3-005',ch:3,lo:'FL-3.2.1',k:'K1',q:'What can happen when stakeholders are not involved throughout development?',o:['The product may not match what stakeholders wanted, causing rework','The product becomes cheaper because stakeholders leave the team alone','Defects are found sooner because there is less discussion','Requirements stay stable throughout the whole project'],a:0,why:'Correct: lack of involvement can lead to delivering the wrong thing, with costly rework and delays. Wrong: the other options describe the opposite of the likely outcome.'},
 {id:'c3-006',ch:3,lo:'FL-3.2.3',k:'K1',q:'Which review role decides what is to be reviewed and provides resources such as staff and time?',o:['Author','Manager','Scribe','Moderator'],a:1,why:'Correct: the manager decides what is reviewed and provides resources. Wrong: the author creates and fixes the work product; the scribe records information; the moderator facilitates the meeting.'},
 {id:'c3-007',ch:3,lo:'FL-3.2.3',k:'K1',q:'Which role ensures review meetings run effectively, including mediation, time management and a safe environment?',o:['Scribe','Author','Moderator (facilitator)','Reviewer'],a:2,why:'Correct: the moderator (facilitator) runs the meeting effectively. Wrong: the scribe records anomalies and decisions; the author created the work product; reviewers examine the work product.'},
 {id:'c3-008',ch:3,lo:'FL-3.2.3',k:'K1',q:'Which role collates anomalies from reviewers and records decisions made in the review meeting?',o:['Manager','Author','Review leader','Scribe (recorder)'],a:3,why:'Correct: the scribe (recorder) collates anomalies and records review information. Wrong: the manager decides what to review and provides resources; the author creates and fixes the work product; the review leader takes overall responsibility and organises the review.'},
 {id:'c3-009',ch:3,lo:'FL-3.2.3',k:'K1',q:'Which responsibility belongs to the review leader?',o:['Taking overall responsibility and deciding who is involved, when and where','Mediating disagreements during meetings and keeping the discussion safe','Creating the work product and fixing it after the review feedback','Recording the decisions made in the meeting and collating anomalies'],a:0,why:'Correct: the review leader has overall responsibility and organises participants, time and place. Wrong: mediation belongs to the moderator; creating and fixing the product is the author; recording decisions is the scribe.'},
 {id:'c3-010',ch:3,lo:'FL-3.2.5',k:'K1',q:'Which factor contributes to a successful review?',o:['Reviewing the whole work product in one long session','Clear objectives and measurable exit criteria','Withholding review feedback from the authors','Keeping management out of the review process'],a:1,why:'Correct: clear objectives and measurable exit criteria help success. Wrong: small chunks keep concentration, so one long session hurts; feedback helps authors improve; management support is a success factor.'},
 {id:'c3-011',ch:3,lo:'FL-3.2.5',k:'K1',q:'Why is it a success factor to conduct reviews in small chunks?',o:['The author can then carry out the whole review alone','A meeting is no longer needed for any review','Reviewers do not lose concentration while reviewing','Exit criteria are no longer needed for the review'],a:2,why:'Correct: small chunks keep reviewers focused. Wrong: chunk size does not let the author review alone, does not remove the need for meetings where they are held, and does not remove the need for exit criteria.'},
 {id:'c3-012',ch:3,lo:'FL-3.1.2',k:'K2',q:'A review finds an ambiguous requirement before any code exists. Which best explains the value of this?',o:['Later defects become harmless because the early ones are fixed','Dynamic testing is no longer needed for this product','The requirement may be ignored, since reviews are only advisory','The defect is fixed cheaply before it spreads into design and code'],a:3,why:'Correct: finding defects early is cheaper than fixing them after they spread. Wrong: other defects do not become harmless; dynamic testing is still needed; the ambiguity must be resolved, not ignored.'},
 {id:'c3-013',ch:3,lo:'FL-3.1.2',k:'K2',q:'Which is a value of reviewing requirements?',o:['It uncovers inconsistencies, ambiguities, omissions and redundancies','It replaces test execution, so later quality checks can safely be skipped','It finds failures that occur in the live system','It proves that the software meets its performance targets'],a:0,why:'Correct: reviews find such problems and improve communication. Wrong: reviews complement test execution rather than replace it; failures appear only when software runs; proving performance targets needs dynamic testing.'},
 {id:'c3-014',ch:3,lo:'FL-3.1.3',k:'K2',q:'Which defect type is typically found more easily by static testing than by dynamic testing?',o:['Slow response times under heavy load','Unreachable code','A crash when two users edit one record','A wrong total shown after checkout'],a:1,why:'Correct: unreachable code can be found by inspecting or analysing the code. Wrong: slow response times, crashes and wrong totals appear as failures when the software runs, which suits dynamic testing.'},
 {id:'c3-015',ch:3,lo:'FL-3.1.3',k:'K2',q:'Which statement best compares static and dynamic testing?',o:['Both approaches find exactly the same types of defects, so doing one of them is enough','Dynamic testing applies only to documents, while static testing needs the running software','Static finds defects directly in work products; dynamic uses execution to reveal failures','Static testing needs the software to be executed, while dynamic testing does not need that'],a:2,why:'Correct: static finds defects directly without execution, dynamic observes failures during execution. Wrong: the two find different types of defects; saying dynamic testing applies only to documents reverses them, and saying static testing needs execution does too.'},
 {id:'c3-016',ch:3,lo:'FL-3.1.3',k:'K2',q:'Which activity is dynamic testing?',o:['Using a tool to list unused variables in source code','Reviewing a user story for contradictions','Checking source code against naming standards','Running the app with 1000 simulated users and measuring response times'],a:3,why:'Correct: it executes the software. Wrong: finding unused variables, reviewing a story and checking naming standards all examine work products without executing them, so they are static.'},
 {id:'c3-017',ch:3,lo:'FL-3.2.2',k:'K2',q:'Each reviewer inspects the work product alone, logging anomalies, recommendations and questions. Which review activity is this?',o:['Individual review','Review initiation','Planning','Communication and analysis'],a:0,why:'Correct: that is the individual review activity. Wrong: review initiation prepares everyone and everything; planning defines scope and criteria; communication and analysis discusses the anomalies afterwards.'},
 {id:'c3-018',ch:3,lo:'FL-3.2.2',k:'K2',q:'Which pairing of review activity and content is correct?',o:['Fixing and reporting: deciding the scope and the exit criteria of the review','Review initiation: making sure participants have the product and know their roles','Individual review: writing defect reports and tracking each fix to closure','Planning: reviewers log anomalies, recommendations and questions alone'],a:1,why:'Correct: initiation makes sure participants and materials are ready. Wrong: scope and exit criteria are defined in planning; defect reports and fix tracking belong to fixing and reporting; logging anomalies alone is individual review.'},
 {id:'c3-019',ch:3,lo:'FL-3.2.2',k:'K2',q:'Why must anomalies be analysed and discussed during communication and analysis?',o:['The author has to fix every anomaly immediately, whatever the reviewers think','Every anomaly is automatically a defect, so each is sent straight to the author','An anomaly is not always a defect, so its status and follow-up must be decided','Individual review is normally skipped, so everything must be settled there'],a:2,why:'Correct: anomalies may turn out not to be defects, so decisions are needed. Wrong: not every anomaly must be fixed immediately; not every anomaly is a defect; individual review is a normal activity.'},
 {id:'c3-020',ch:3,lo:'FL-3.2.4',k:'K2',q:'Which review type is led by the author and may aim to educate reviewers and gain consensus?',o:['Inspection','Informal review','Technical review','Walkthrough'],a:3,why:'Correct: a walkthrough is led by the author. Wrong: an inspection follows the complete formal process and the author cannot be its review leader; an informal review follows no defined process; a technical review is led by a moderator with technically qualified reviewers.'},
 {id:'c3-021',ch:3,lo:'FL-3.2.4',k:'K2',q:'Which description fits an inspection rather than the other review types?',o:['It is led by the author, and reviewers need not prepare beforehand','It follows the complete review process and collects metrics to improve the process','It follows no defined process and needs no formal documented output','It is done by technically qualified reviewers to settle a technical problem'],a:1,why:'Correct: inspections are the most formal type, following the complete generic process with metrics collected for improvement. Wrong: author-led with optional preparation describes a walkthrough; no defined process describes an informal review; qualified reviewers settling a technical problem describes a technical review.'},
 {id:'c3-022',ch:3,lo:'FL-3.2.4',k:'K2',q:'Which statement correctly compares an informal review with an inspection?',o:['They are equally formal and differ only in how many people attend the review','Informal reviews follow no defined process; inspections follow the full process and collect metrics','Only informal reviews use a scribe, because inspections rely on the author alone','Informal reviews need a documented process and metrics, while inspections need neither'],a:1,why:'Correct: informal reviews have no defined process, inspections are the most formal. Wrong: formality differs greatly, not just attendance; inspections do use a scribe and the author cannot act as scribe; claiming black-box tests come from code reverses the two.'}
);
EXAMDATA.terms.push(
 {"t":"Component testing","ch":2,"d":"Testing a single piece of the software on its own, usually by developers, often using stubs or drivers to stand in for other parts."},
 {"t":"System integration testing","ch":2,"d":"Testing the connections between your system and other systems or external services, like a till talking to a payment provider."},
 {"t":"Acceptance testing","ch":2,"d":"Testing to confirm the software is ready to be deployed and does what users and the business need. Forms include UAT, OAT, contractual, regulatory, alpha and beta."},
 {"t":"Confirmation testing","ch":2,"d":"Re-running a failed test after a fix to check the defect is really gone."},
 {"t":"Regression testing","ch":2,"d":"Testing that a change did not accidentally break something that worked before."},
 {"t":"Maintenance testing","ch":2,"d":"Testing done after software is live and changes through modification, migration or retirement."},
 {"t":"Impact analysis","ch":2,"d":"Working out what a change affects, including side effects, so you know which areas to retest."},
 {"t":"Shift-left","ch":2,"d":"Starting testing activities earlier in the lifecycle, without neglecting later testing."},
 {"t":"Test-driven development (TDD)","ch":2,"d":"A test-first approach where developers write tests first, then code to pass them, then refactor."},
 {"t":"Retrospective","ch":2,"d":"A team meeting to look back at what went well and what did not, and to agree improvements."},
 {"t":"Static analysis","ch":3,"d":"Using a tool to examine code or models for problems without running them, such as unused variables or unreachable code."},
 {"t":"Review","ch":3,"d":"A static test where people examine a work product to find anomalies, using a defined process and roles."},
 {"t":"Walkthrough","ch":3,"d":"A review led by the author, used to educate reviewers, build consensus and find anomalies."},
 {"t":"Inspection","ch":3,"d":"The most formal review type, with defined roles and process, and metrics used to improve both the product and the process."}
);
